  "briefing": [
    "Penhallow House, Cornwall. Saturday 27 July 1963, ten o’clock in the morning.",
    "For twenty years the village has called Lady Hester Penhallow the Glasshouse Widow. Since the telegram from Burma in 1943 she has worn black, veil and all, and lived among the plants her husband sent home — above all the moon orchid, which had never once flowered in England. Last night, for one night only, it bloomed.",
    "She kept her vigil alone, locked in the glasshouse, and promised to ring the bell when it opened. At 11.32 the bell rang, and four people on the terrace saw her through the glass, lamp in hand beside the great white flower. At a quarter to six this morning the gardener found her dead at the foot of a stepladder.",
    "On Tuesday she wrote asking you to come: someone in her family had done ‘something criminal’. Her letter reached you too late. Sergeant Polglase of St Austell has a theory and a suspect, and has given you the morning before the coroner’s officer arrives.",
    "Find out who killed Lady Penhallow, how it was done and why — and be ready to prove it."
  ],
