  "epilogue": [
    "Celia Penhallow held out for two days. Then Sergeant Polglase laid Dr. Ellery’s photograph in front of her: the tall black figure with the lamp held high. She looked at it for a long time, and then she said, quite calmly, ‘I always did play to the gallery.’",
    "Hester had told her at six o’clock: Julian had forged her name on a mortgage of Home Farm, and in the morning the deed would go to Mr. Kittow, and from Mr. Kittow to the police. At twenty to eleven Celia went down to the glasshouse to beg. Hester said that Julian would answer for it, as Edmund would have wished. Her stick was leaning against the bench.",
    "Afterwards Celia did what eleven years in repertory had taught her: she waited for her cue. She sat with the body for three-quarters of an hour while the moon orchid opened beside it. Then she put on the veil and the shawl, rang the bell and gave the performance of her life to an audience of four. Afterwards she dressed Hester in them again, locked the door so that no doctor would see the body before morning, burned the deed in the stokehole, and went in to drink champagne.",
    "She had studied everything about Hester Penhallow except the one thing that mattered. Hester would never have let that flower open and close untouched.",
    "Julian Penhallow was sent to prison for three years for forgery. The new will was never signed, so Penhallow came to him after all, and from his cell he gave it to the National Trust, gardens, glasshouse and all. Marian Ellery turned down Mr. Lindqvist’s twenty thousand dollars and took the seed to Kew. Edwin Kittow kept the ring he had bought in Truro for September.",
    "In November a single green pod ripened on the moon orchid. The first seedlings flowered at Kew in the spring of 1971. Jory Trevenna travelled up in his Sunday suit to see them, and chose their name: they are registered as ‘Glasshouse Widow’. On the last Friday of every July he still sits up all night in the glasshouse. Somebody ought to, he says."
